From: Yocto build fix
Subject: [PATCH] autoapp: declare the real touch surface size for AA to scale against

InputService::fillFeatures() declares this board's touch surface size to
the phone via touch_screen_config, using getTouchscreenGeometry() (this
same screenGeometry rect). Paired with 0011, InputDevice no longer scales
touch coordinates against AA's video resolution on the host side at all --
it sends touchPoint.pos() through unscaled, on the theory (confirmed on
real hardware, see 0011's commit message) that AA maps the declared touch
surface to its own rendered video resolution internally, the same way
essentially every touchscreen protocol separates "declare your device's
coordinate space once" from "report raw coordinates in it".

For that declaration to be accurate, this rect must match the actual
coordinate space Qt's evdevtouch reports touchPoint.pos() in, which is the
*full screen* (1280x720, confirmed via /sys/class/graphics/fb0's
virtual_size) -- not QtVideoOutput's smaller (1024x600) video widget. Two
earlier attempts at this same rect tried to make it a *scaling divisor*
instead -- first the widget's size (assuming a client-side scale-up was
needed to invert the widget's render-time squish), then reverted back to
the full screen (assuming Qt's own normalization already accounted for
everything). Both were still doing *some* client-side scaling, which
turned out to be the wrong model entirely: real on-device testing (tapping
Contacts, a call icon, and other UI elements with each variant) showed
each still landing on the wrong element, until scaling was removed
entirely in favor of raw passthrough (0011) with this rect used purely as
the accompanying declaration. That combination was the first one confirmed
by direct on-screen testing to actually open the right icon.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [oe-specific: declares this board's real
touch surface size for AA's own internal coordinate mapping]

diff --git a/src/autoapp/Service/ServiceFactory.cpp b/src/autoapp/Service/ServiceFactory.cpp
--- a/src/autoapp/Service/ServiceFactory.cpp
+++ b/src/autoapp/Service/ServiceFactory.cpp
@@ -120,17 +120,18 @@
         break;
     }
 
-    // screenGeometry here must match the area QtVideoOutput actually renders
-    // into (see kPanelVisibleGeometry in QtVideoOutput.cpp), NOT the full
-    // primaryScreen()->geometry() -- this board's HDMI scanout (1280x720) is
-    // larger than the physical panel's real visible/native area (1024x600),
-    // so using the full screen here would scale touch coordinates onto a
-    // canvas bigger than what's actually shown, misplacing every touch.
-    // 1024x600 also happens to be this exact panel's own touchscreen
-    // hardware calibration range (confirmed via Qt's evdevtouch device
-    // discovery log: "min X: 0 max X: 1024", "min Y: 0 max Y: 600"), so this
-    // makes the touch transform a clean 1:1 identity mapping.
-    QRect screenGeometry(0, 0, 1024, 600);
+    // This rect is now purely a declaration for InputService::fillFeatures'
+    // touch_screen_config, not a scaling divisor (see InputDevice's
+    // raw-passthrough touch change) -- it must match the real coordinate
+    // space Qt's evdevtouch reports touch in, which is the full screen, not
+    // QtVideoOutput's smaller on-screen video widget size. Two earlier
+    // attempts at using this rect as a client-side scaling divisor (widget
+    // size, then full screen) both still landed touches on the wrong
+    // element on real hardware; only removing client-side scaling entirely
+    // in favor of raw passthrough plus this accurate declaration was
+    // confirmed correct by on-device testing (tapping application icons).
+    QScreen* screen = QGuiApplication::primaryScreen();
+    QRect screenGeometry = screen == nullptr ? QRect(0, 0, 1, 1) : screen->geometry();
     projection::IInputDevice::Pointer inputDevice(std::make_shared<projection::InputDevice>(*QApplication::instance(), configuration_, std::move(screenGeometry), std::move(videoGeometry)));
 
     return std::make_shared<InputService>(ioService_, messenger, std::move(inputDevice));
